  [38;5;153mSERVICE   VERSION   READY   DEPLOYED           HEALTH[39m
  [38;5;153mbilling   4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   failing: db-connection[39m
  [38;5;153msearch    1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   failing: disk-space, index-freshness[39m
  [38;5;153mwebhooks  0.6.3     1/1     2026-09-30 06:55   ok[39m
  You see which service and which check at a glance, and the output stays one line per service. Upside: easy to [38;5;153mgrep[39m.
  Downside: you can't see [3mwhy[0m a check failed, like "timeout after 5s".

  [1mB. Column A plus an indented detail line under each failing check.[0m
  [38;5;153mbilling   4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   failing: db-connection[39m
    [38;5;153mdb-connection: timeout after 5s[39m
  [38;5;153msearch    1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   failing: disk-space, index-freshness[39m
    [38;5;153mdisk-space: 91% used[39m
    [38;5;153mindex-freshness: last update 47 min ago[39m
  You get the full diagnosis in one command, but rows are no longer strictly one per service. The existing "keeps
  snapshot order" test and anyone parsing rows would need to skip the detail lines.

  [1mC. Column A plus a "N failing" count in the heading[0m, e.g. [38;5;153mproduction: 10 services (3 failing health checks), as of …[39m.
  This works with either A or B.

  [1mRecommendation: A + C.[0m Answering "which services are failing" is the actual request. The heading count lets you check
  health with one look at the first line, and the output stays one line per service. Adding details later (say, behind a
  [38;5;153m--verbose[39m flag) is cheap. Undoing a multi-line format once people depend on it is not.
